(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "8.5", title: "Logic gates and Boolean logic", q: "How do simple switches combine to make decisions?", lessons: [
{ t: "True and false: logic in everyday rules", o: "I can describe everyday rules using true and false.",
  k: ["logic: reasoning with statements that are either true or false", "Boolean: having only two possible values, true or false (1 or 0)", "statement: a sentence that is either true or false"],
  d: ["'You may go out if you have finished your homework AND tidied your room.' You have done the homework only. Can you go out?", "No. Both parts must be true."],
  s: ["True or false|A statement is either true or false, never in between|'The door is open' is a statement; 'Open the door' is not|True is written 1 and false is written 0",
      "Combining statements|AND: both must be true|OR: at least one must be true|NOT: the opposite",
      "Why computers care|A switch is on or off: 1 or 0|Circuits that combine switches make decisions|Every calculation a processor does is built from these"],
  a: "Rewrite the ten everyday rules on the sheet using AND, OR and NOT, then decide the outcome for the situations given. Write three rules of your own from school life. Support: choose AND or OR for the first six rules. Stretch: write a rule that needs all three of AND, OR and NOT.",
  w: [["What two values can a Boolean have?", "True or false (1 or 0)."], ["Which of these is a statement: 'It is raining' or 'Is it raining?'", "'It is raining'."], ["Rule: a discount is given if you are a student OR over 65. Sam is 70 and not a student. Discount?", "Yes."], ["Rule: entry if you have a ticket AND are NOT carrying food. Ali has a ticket and a sandwich. Entry?", "No."], ["Explain why 'OR' in logic is different from 'tea or coffee?' in everyday speech.", "In logic, OR is true when either or both are true. In everyday speech it often means only one of them."]],
  x: ["Write true as a binary digit.", "1."] },
{ t: "NOT, AND and OR gates", o: "I can draw the NOT, AND and OR gates and complete their truth tables.",
  k: ["logic gate: an electronic component that makes a decision from its inputs", "truth table: a table showing the output for every possible combination of inputs", "input: a signal going into a gate, either 0 or 1"],
  d: ["A lamp has two switches in a row and both must be on for it to light. Which word describes this: AND or OR?", "AND."],
  s: ["NOT gate|One input, one output|The output is the opposite of the input|Symbol: a triangle with a small circle on the point",
      "AND gate|Output is 1 only when both inputs are 1|Symbol: flat back, rounded front, like a letter D|Truth table outputs for 00, 01, 10, 11: 0, 0, 0, 1",
      "OR gate|Output is 1 when at least one input is 1|Symbol: curved back, pointed front|Truth table outputs: 0, 1, 1, 1"],
  a: "Draw each gate symbol three times from memory, complete the truth tables for NOT, AND and OR, and match the six real-life situations to a gate. Check with the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit. Support: use the symbol cards while you draw. Stretch: write the truth table for a three-input AND gate.",
  w: [["How many inputs does a NOT gate have?", "One."], ["When is the output of an AND gate 1?", "Only when both inputs are 1."], ["Complete the OR truth table outputs for inputs 00, 01, 10, 11.", "0, 1, 1, 1."], ["A is 1 and B is 0. What is A AND B? What is A OR B?", "0 and 1."], ["Explain how you tell an AND gate symbol from an OR gate symbol.", "The AND gate has a flat back and a rounded front. The OR gate has a curved back and a pointed front."]],
  x: ["What is NOT 1?", "0."] },
{ t: "Lab: human logic gates and the simulator", o: "I can model logic gates and check my predictions with a simulator.",
  k: ["simulator: a program that imitates a real system", "predict: to say what will happen before testing", "signal: a 0 or a 1 passed along a wire"],
  d: ["Predict: A = 1, B = 1. What is the output of an OR gate?", "1."],
  s: ["Human gates|Two students are inputs: standing is 1, sitting is 0|A third student is the gate and follows its rule|The class checks the output",
      "The simulator|Open Logic gates in CS Toolkit|Load an example such as X = A AND B|Switch the inputs and watch the wire colours: green carries 1",
      "Predict first|Write your prediction for every row|Then switch the inputs to check|Tick or correct each row"],
  a: "Take part in the human gates activity for NOT, AND and OR. Then, in the simulator, complete predicted and actual truth tables for X = A AND B, X = A OR B and X = NOT A, and try the practice mode for one of them. Support: complete the AND table with a partner. Stretch: type X = NOT (A AND B) and describe how its table differs from AND.",
  w: [["In the human gate activity, what did standing up represent?", "1 (true)."], ["In the simulator, what does a green wire mean?", "It is carrying a 1."], ["What output did X = A OR B give for A = 0, B = 0?", "0."], ["How many rows does a truth table for two inputs have?", "4."], ["Explain why predicting before testing helps you learn.", "It makes you apply the rule yourself, and a wrong prediction shows exactly what you have misunderstood."]],
  x: ["What was your practice-mode score?", "A score out of 4."] },
{ t: "NAND, NOR and XOR gates", o: "I can complete truth tables for NAND, NOR and XOR gates.",
  k: ["NAND: NOT AND; the output is 0 only when both inputs are 1", "NOR: NOT OR; the output is 1 only when both inputs are 0", "XOR: exclusive OR; the output is 1 when the inputs are different"],
  d: ["Take the AND truth table outputs 0, 0, 0, 1 and flip every one. What do you get?", "1, 1, 1, 0."],
  s: ["NAND and NOR|NAND is AND followed by NOT: outputs 1, 1, 1, 0|NOR is OR followed by NOT: outputs 1, 0, 0, 0|Their symbols are AND and OR with a small circle on the output",
      "XOR|1 when the inputs are different: outputs 0, 1, 1, 0|This is 'one or the other but not both'|Symbol: an OR gate with an extra curved line at the back",
      "Six gates|NOT, AND, OR, NAND, NOR, XOR|The small circle always means 'inverted'|These are the gates in the IGCSE syllabus"],
  a: "Complete truth tables for NAND, NOR and XOR and draw each symbol. Sort the six gate cards by matching symbol, name and truth table. Support: build NAND and NOR by flipping the AND and OR columns. Stretch: show that a NAND gate with both inputs joined together behaves as a NOT gate.",
  w: [["What does the small circle on a gate's output mean?", "The output is inverted (NOT)."], ["Give the NAND outputs for inputs 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 1, 1, 0."], ["Give the NOR outputs for inputs 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 0, 0, 0."], ["Give the XOR outputs for inputs 00, 01, 10, 11.", "0, 1, 1, 0."], ["Explain the difference between OR and XOR.", "OR gives 1 when either or both inputs are 1. XOR gives 1 only when exactly one input is 1."]],
  x: ["Which gate gives 1 only when both inputs are 0?", "NOR."] },
{ t: "Truth tables for two-gate circuits", o: "I can complete a truth table for a circuit with two gates.",
  k: ["logic circuit: gates connected so that the output of one is the input of another", "intermediate output: the output of a gate inside the circuit, before the final output", "combination: one possible set of input values"],
  d: ["A = 1, B = 0. Work out NOT B, then A AND (NOT B).", "NOT B is 1, so the answer is 1."],
  s: ["Circuits|The output of one gate feeds the input of the next|Work from the inputs towards the output|Never try to do it all in one step",
      "Use an extra column|List every input combination: 00, 01, 10, 11|Add a column for each intermediate output|Then fill in the final output",
      "Example: X = A AND (NOT B)|NOT B column: 1, 0, 1, 0|X column: 0, 0, 1, 0|Only A = 1, B = 0 gives 1"],
  a: "Complete truth tables, with intermediate columns, for the eight two-gate circuits on the sheet. Support: the intermediate columns are labelled for you. Stretch: find two different circuits that have exactly the same truth table.",
  w: [["What is an intermediate output?", "The output of a gate inside the circuit, before the final output."], ["Give the outputs of X = A AND (NOT B) for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11.", "0, 0, 1, 0."], ["Give the outputs of X = NOT (A OR B) for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 0, 0, 0."], ["Give the outputs of X = (NOT A) OR B for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 1, 0, 1."], ["Explain why adding a column for each intermediate output reduces mistakes.", "Each column needs only one gate's rule, so there is less to hold in your head and errors are easier to spot."]],
  x: ["In what order should input combinations be listed for two inputs?", "00, 01, 10, 11 (counting in binary)."] },
{ t: "Lab: predict, then check circuits", o: "I can predict the truth table of a circuit and verify it.",
  k: ["verify: to check that something is correct", "expression: a logic circuit written in words and letters, such as X = A AND B", "bracket: shows which part of an expression is worked out first"],
  d: ["Why do we write (A AND B) OR C with brackets?", "To show that the AND is worked out first."],
  s: ["Method|Read the expression|Complete your predicted truth table on paper|Type the expression into the simulator and compare",
      "Reading the simulator|The circuit is drawn from the expression|Click a row of its truth table to set the inputs|Follow the green wires through each gate",
      "If you were wrong|Find the first row that differs|Check that row's intermediate values|Which gate rule did you misapply?"],
  a: "For each of the six expressions, complete a predicted truth table, then check it in the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit and correct any errors. Use practice mode on two of them and record your scores. Support: predict the first three with a partner. Stretch: write an expression using three different gates, predict its table and check it.",
  w: [["What does verify mean?", "To check that something is correct."], ["What are brackets for in a logic expression?", "They show which part is worked out first."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A NAND B) for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 1, 1, 0."], ["Give the outputs of X = NOT (A XOR B) for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 0, 0, 1."], ["Explain what to do when your prediction and the simulator disagree.", "Find the first row that differs and check each intermediate value to see which gate rule was applied wrongly."]],
  x: ["How many of your six predictions were fully correct?", "A number out of 6."] },
{ t: "From circuit to logic expression", o: "I can write the logic expression for a circuit diagram.",
  k: ["logic expression: a written description of a circuit using gate names and input letters", "label: a letter given to an input or output", "trace: to follow a path through a circuit"],
  d: ["Inputs A and B go into an OR gate. Write this as an expression.", "A OR B."],
  s: ["Method|Start at the inputs on the left|Write the expression for each gate's output beside it|Carry that expression into the next gate",
      "Brackets|Put brackets round each gate's expression before using it in the next|An AND gate fed by (A OR B) and C gives (A OR B) AND C|A NOT gate puts NOT in front: NOT (A AND B)",
      "Finish|Write X = at the start|Check: one operator for each gate|Count the gates and the operators"],
  a: "Write the logic expression for each of the ten circuit diagrams, labelling each gate's output as you go. Support: the first four have the gate outputs labelled. Stretch: draw a circuit with four gates, then write its expression.",
  w: [["A and B go into an AND gate. The output goes into a NOT gate. Write the expression.", "X = NOT (A AND B)."], ["A goes into a NOT gate. Its output and B go into an OR gate. Write the expression.", "X = (NOT A) OR B."], ["A and B go into an OR gate. Its output and C go into an AND gate. Write the expression.", "X = (A OR B) AND C."], ["How can you check you have not missed a gate?", "Count the gates in the circuit and the operators in the expression: they should match."], ["Explain why brackets matter, using A OR B AND C as an example.", "(A OR B) AND C and A OR (B AND C) are different circuits with different truth tables, so brackets are needed to show which is meant."]],
  x: ["Which single gate is the same as NOT (A OR B)?", "NOR."] },
{ t: "From expression to circuit", o: "I can draw a logic circuit from a logic expression.",
  k: ["draw: to produce a diagram with the correct symbols", "innermost bracket: the part of the expression that is worked out first", "wire: a line connecting the output of one gate to the input of another"],
  d: ["In (A AND B) OR C, which gate comes first?", "The AND gate."],
  s: ["Method|Find the innermost brackets: draw those gates first, on the left|Feed their outputs into the next gate|The last operator is the gate that gives X",
      "Example: X = (A AND B) OR (NOT C)|Draw an AND gate with inputs A and B|Draw a NOT gate with input C|Feed both outputs into an OR gate that gives X",
      "Neat diagrams|Inputs on the left, output on the right|Wires straight, with as few crossings as possible|Label every input and the output"],
  a: "Draw the circuit for each of the eight expressions using the correct symbols. Check two of them in the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit. Support: arrange the gate cards before drawing. Stretch: draw X = (A NAND B) XOR (B NOR C) and complete its truth table.",
  w: [["Which gate do you draw first for X = (A OR B) AND C?", "The OR gate."], ["How many gates are needed for X = (A AND B) OR (NOT C)?", "Three: AND, NOT and OR."], ["Describe the circuit for X = NOT (A AND B).", "A and B go into an AND gate, and its output goes into a NOT gate that gives X."], ["Which gate gives the final output of X = (A XOR B) AND (NOT C)?", "The AND gate."], ["Explain how to decide the order in which to draw the gates.", "Work from the innermost brackets outwards. The last operation in the expression is the final gate."]],
  x: ["Where do the inputs go on a neat circuit diagram?", "On the left."] },
{ t: "Lab: write, swap, draw, check", o: "I can move between circuits, expressions and truth tables in any direction.",
  k: ["equivalent: having the same truth table", "check: to compare with a correct answer", "representation: one way of showing the same thing"],
  d: ["Name the three ways we can show the same piece of logic.", "A circuit diagram, a logic expression and a truth table."],
  s: ["The challenge|Write an expression with two or three gates|Your partner draws the circuit|You both complete the truth table",
      "Check|Type the expression into the simulator|Compare the circuit and the table|Agree what went wrong, if anything",
      "Three representations|Expression, circuit and truth table describe the same logic|An exam question may give any one and ask for another|Practise every direction"],
  a: "In pairs, complete six rounds of write, swap, draw and check, increasing the difficulty each round. Record each expression, circuit and table. Support: use expressions with two gates. Stretch: find two expressions that look different but are equivalent, and prove it with their truth tables.",
  w: [["What does equivalent mean for two circuits?", "They have the same truth table."], ["Write an expression that needs exactly three gates.", "Any valid example, such as X = (A AND B) OR (NOT C)."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A OR B) AND (NOT A) for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11.", "0, 1, 0, 0."], ["Which representation shows the output for every input combination?", "The truth table."], ["Explain how you could prove that two different circuits do the same job.", "Complete the truth table for each. If the output columns are identical, the circuits are equivalent."]],
  x: ["Which direction do you find hardest: to circuit, to expression or to truth table?", "A named direction."] },
{ t: "Three-input circuits and eight-row tables", o: "I can complete a truth table for a circuit with three inputs.",
  k: ["three-input: having inputs A, B and C", "row: one input combination in a truth table", "power of 2: the number of rows is 2 to the power of the number of inputs"],
  d: ["Two inputs need 4 rows. How many rows do three inputs need? Why?", "8, because each extra input doubles the number of combinations."],
  s: ["Eight rows|000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111|It is counting in binary from 0 to 7|A column: four 0s then four 1s. B: two and two. C: alternating",
      "Same method|Add a column for each intermediate output|Work through one gate at a time|Check each row twice",
      "Example: X = (A AND B) OR C|A AND B column: 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1|X column: 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1"],
  a: "Complete eight-row truth tables for the six three-input circuits, with intermediate columns. Check two with the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit. Support: the input columns are filled in for you. Stretch: how many rows would four and five inputs need? Complete a four-input table.",
  w: [["How many rows does a truth table with three inputs have?", "8."], ["Write the eight input combinations in order.", "000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A AND B) OR C for the eight rows.", "0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A OR B) AND (NOT C) for the eight rows.", "0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0."], ["Explain why a truth table with n inputs has 2 to the power n rows.", "Each input can be 0 or 1, and each extra input doubles the number of possible combinations."]],
  x: ["How many rows would a five-input truth table need?", "32."] },
{ t: "From a problem statement to a circuit", o: "I can design a logic circuit to solve a described problem.",
  k: ["problem statement: a description in words of what a system must do", "condition: something that is either true (1) or false (0)", "design: to plan a solution"],
  d: ["'The light comes on if it is dark and someone is in the room.' Which gate?", "AND."],
  s: ["Method|Give each condition a letter and say what 1 means|Find the words and, or, not in the statement|Write the expression, then draw the circuit",
      "Example|An alarm X sounds if the system is armed (A = 1) and either the door (D = 1) or the window (W = 1) is open|X = A AND (D OR W)|Two gates: OR, then AND",
      "Check with a truth table|Armed, both closed: 0|Armed, door open: 1|Not armed, door open: 0. Does that match the description?"],
  a: "For each of the five problem statements, define the inputs, write the expression, draw the circuit and complete the truth table. Support: the inputs are defined and the key words are underlined. Stretch: design a circuit for a seat-belt warning with three inputs, and justify each gate.",
  w: [["What is the first step in turning a problem statement into a circuit?", "Give each condition a letter and say what 1 means."], ["Write the expression: a fan F turns on if it is hot (H) or humid (M).", "F = H OR M."], ["Write the expression: a door D unlocks if a card is valid (C) and the alarm is not set (A).", "D = C AND (NOT A)."], ["Give the outputs of X = A AND (D OR W) for the eight rows of A, D, W.", "0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1."], ["Explain why you should check your circuit with a truth table against the problem statement.", "The table shows the output for every situation, so you can confirm each one matches what the description requires."]],
  x: ["Which words in a problem statement tell you which gates to use?", "and, or, not (also 'either', 'both', 'unless')."] },
{ t: "Lab: design an alarm or greenhouse controller", o: "I can design, build and test a logic circuit for a real situation.",
  k: ["controller: a system that switches devices on or off according to rules", "sensor: a device that gives a 1 or 0 depending on what it detects", "specification: an exact description of what the system must do"],
  d: ["A greenhouse heater should come on when it is cold and it is not daytime. Write the expression using C and D.", "H = C AND (NOT D)."],
  s: ["Choose|Alarm system: armed switch, door sensor, window sensor, panic button|Or greenhouse: temperature, light and moisture sensors controlling a heater and a pump|Read the specification on your card",
      "Design|Define each input and output|Write an expression for each output|Draw the circuit",
      "Test|Build it in the simulator by typing each expression|Complete the truth table|Check every row against the specification"],
  a: "Design the controller on your specification card: define inputs, write the expressions, draw the circuits and test them in the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit, recording the truth tables. Support: design the heater output only. Stretch: add a manual override input that forces the output on.",
  w: [["List your inputs and say what 1 means for each.", "A list of defined inputs."], ["Write the expression for your main output.", "A valid expression."], ["How many gates does your circuit use?", "A number matching the expression."], ["Give one row of your truth table and explain why the output is correct.", "A row with a reason linked to the specification."], ["Explain how the override input changes your expression.", "The original expression is combined with the override using OR, for example X = (original) OR M."]],
  x: ["Did every row of your truth table match the specification?", "Yes, or which row needed fixing."] },
{ t: "From truth table to expression", o: "I can write a logic expression from a truth table.",
  k: ["sum of products: an expression made of AND terms joined by OR", "term: one part of an expression", "minterm: an AND term that is 1 for exactly one row of the truth table"],
  d: ["A truth table has output 1 only for A = 1, B = 0. Write an expression that is 1 for that row only.", "A AND (NOT B)."],
  s: ["Method|Find every row where the output is 1|For each, write an AND term: use the letter if the input is 1, NOT the letter if it is 0|Join the terms with OR",
      "Example: XOR|Output is 1 for A = 0, B = 1 and for A = 1, B = 0|Terms: (NOT A AND B) and (A AND NOT B)|X = (NOT A AND B) OR (A AND NOT B)",
      "Try it|In the simulator, use 'From truth table to expression'|Set the outputs and read the expression|It always works, though it is not always the shortest"],
  a: "Write a sum-of-products expression for each of the six truth tables, then check them with the truth-table tool in the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit. Support: start with tables that have a single 1 in the output. Stretch: for a table with three 1s, find a shorter expression that gives the same outputs.",
  w: [["What is the first step in writing an expression from a truth table?", "Find every row where the output is 1."], ["Write the term for the row A = 1, B = 1.", "A AND B."], ["Write the term for the row A = 0, B = 0.", "(NOT A) AND (NOT B)."], ["A table has output 1 for AB = 01 and AB = 10 only. Write the expression.", "X = (NOT A AND B) OR (A AND NOT B)."], ["A table has output 1 for AB = 01 and AB = 11. Write the sum-of-products expression, then a simpler one.", "X = (NOT A AND B) OR (A AND B). Simpler: X = B."]],
  x: ["Which gate joins the terms in a sum of products?", "OR."] },
{ t: "Logic in Python: and, or, not", o: "I can connect logic gates to conditions in Python.",
  k: ["Boolean expression: an expression that works out to True or False", "operator: `and`, `or` and `not` in Python", "condition: the Boolean expression tested by an `if` or `while`"],
  d: ["In Unit 8.1 you wrote `if height >= 120 and age >= 8:`. Which logic gate is that?", "AND."],
  s: ["The same logic|Python has `and`, `or`, `not`|True and False instead of 1 and 0|`if armed and (door or window):` is the alarm circuit",
      "Truth tables in code|>> for a in [False, True]:|>>     for b in [False, True]:|>>         print(a, b, a and b)|Prints the AND truth table",
      "Other gates|NAND: `not (a and b)`|NOR: `not (a or b)`|XOR: `a != b`"],
  a: "Write Python programs that print the truth tables for AND, OR, NAND and XOR, then write the alarm rule X = A AND (D OR W) as an if statement that asks the user three yes or no questions. Support: complete the AND program and change one word to make OR. Stretch: print the eight-row truth table for the alarm using three nested loops.",
  w: [["Which Python operator matches the AND gate?", "and"], ["Write NOR in Python using `a` and `b`.", "not (a or b)"], ["What does `True and False` give?", "False."], ["What does `not (True or False)` give?", "False."], ["Explain how a logic circuit and an `if` statement are doing the same job.", "Both combine true or false inputs with AND, OR and NOT to decide an outcome: one in hardware, one in software."]],
  x: ["Write XOR in Python.", "a != b"] },
{ t: "Lab: program the controller in Python", o: "I can implement my logic design in software and compare it with the circuit.",
  k: ["implement: to turn a design into a working system", "hardware: the physical circuit", "software: the program that does the same job"],
  d: ["Your greenhouse heater rule was H = C AND (NOT D). Write it as a Python condition.", "cold and not daytime"],
  s: ["From circuit to code|Each input becomes a variable that is True or False|Each expression becomes a condition|Each output becomes a print or a variable",
      "Getting True or False from the user|>> cold = input('Is it cold? ') == 'y'|The comparison gives True or False|Repeat for each input",
      "Compare|Run the program for every row of your truth table|Do the outputs match the circuit?|Which was easier to change: the circuit or the code?"],
  a: "Write your alarm or greenhouse controller from last week as a Python program. Test it against every row of your truth table and record the results. Support: complete the part-written controller. Stretch: make the program loop until the user types 'quit', printing the output each time.",
  w: [["How is an input from the circuit represented in your program?", "As a variable holding True or False."], ["Write the line that stores True in `door` if the user types y.", "door = input('Is the door open? ') == 'y'"], ["Write the alarm rule as a Python condition.", "armed and (door or window)"], ["Did your program match your truth table for every row?", "Yes, or the row that differed."], ["Give one advantage of doing the logic in software and one of doing it in hardware.", "Software is easy to change. Hardware is very fast and needs no computer or program to run."]],
  x: ["How many rows of your truth table did you test in Python?", "All of them (8)."] },
{ t: "Revision with practice mode", o: "I can recall gate rules and apply them accurately.",
  k: ["revise: to go over learning again", "accuracy: getting answers right", "recall: to bring back from memory"],
  d: ["Write the output columns of AND, OR and XOR from memory.", "0001, 0111, 0110."],
  s: ["Six gates|NOT flips. AND needs both. OR needs at least one|NAND and NOR are AND and OR inverted|XOR is 1 when the inputs differ",
      "Three skills|Truth table from a circuit or expression: use intermediate columns|Expression from a circuit, and circuit from an expression: work through the brackets|Circuit from a problem statement: define inputs, find and, or, not",
      "Practice mode|In the Logic gates page of CS Toolkit, press 'Practice: hide the output column'|Fill in the outputs and check|Aim for full marks on three expressions in a row"],
  a: "Complete the revision sheet, then use practice mode on six expressions of increasing difficulty and record your scores. Support: practise two-input expressions. Stretch: practise four-input expressions with sixteen rows.",
  w: [["Give the NAND outputs for 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 1, 1, 0."], ["Write the expression: A and B into an XOR gate, then into a NOT gate.", "X = NOT (A XOR B)."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A AND B) OR (NOT C) for the eight rows.", "1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1."], ["Write the expression: a pump P runs if the soil is dry (D) and the tank is not empty (E).", "P = D AND (NOT E)."], ["A table has output 1 only for the row ABC = 110. Write the expression.", "X = A AND B AND (NOT C)."]],
  x: ["What was your best practice-mode score on a three-input expression?", "A score out of 8."] },
{ t: "Assessed test", o: "I can show what I know about logic gates and circuits.",
  k: ["test conditions: working alone, in silence, without notes", "symbol: the standard shape for a gate", "complete: fill in every row"],
  d: ["What should you add to a truth table to avoid mistakes?", "A column for each intermediate output."],
  s: ["The test|Gate recall: names, symbols and tables|Two circuits to tabulate|One expression to draw and one design problem",
      "Gaining marks|Use the correct symbols|Show intermediate columns|Label inputs and outputs",
      "Checking|Have you filled in every row?|Does the number of gates match the number of operators?|Does the design match the description?"],
  a: "Complete the written test under test conditions. Support: a symbol sheet without names is provided. Stretch: attempt the extension question on building AND from NAND gates only.",
  w: [["Name the gate whose output is 1 only when both inputs are 1.", "AND."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A NOR B) for 00, 01, 10, 11.", "1, 0, 0, 0."], ["Give the outputs of X = (A OR B) AND (NOT A) for 00, 01, 10, 11.", "0, 1, 0, 0."], ["Describe the circuit for X = (A AND B) OR (NOT C).", "A and B go into an AND gate; C goes into a NOT gate; both outputs go into an OR gate that gives X."], ["A lift moves (M = 1) if the door is closed (D = 1) and a floor button is pressed (B = 1), unless the emergency stop is on (E = 1). Write the expression.", "M = D AND B AND (NOT E)."]],
  x: ["Which question were you least sure about?", "A question number."] },
